From c7689ab739e08b687a38bc910f585ab9b2d1ea12 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?David=20H=C3=A4rdeman?= <david@hardeman.nu>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:12:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix hosts file index collision between --addn-hosts dirs and
 --hostsdir
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Files found in an --addn-hosts directory get their index from
expand_filelist(), which counts on from the largest index in the
--addn-hosts list and doesn't advance daemon->host_index. Files in a
--hostsdir directory get theirs from daemon->host_index
(dyndir_addhosts()), which doesn't know about the former, so files of
both can get the same index.

When such a --hostsdir file changes, cache_remove_uid() then also
removes the names read from the --addn-hosts file with the same index,
and they stay gone until the next SIGHUP. E.g. with
--addn-hosts=/dir1 (containing a.hosts) and --hostsdir=/dir2, creating
/dir2/x.hosts flushes the names from /dir1/a.hosts.
record_source() also logs the wrong file for them. The names are lost
since 2.88, where a changed --hostsdir file has its old names removed
(commits 0017dd7 and 0ba25a0); before, the collision only affected the
logged source.

Allocate the indices in expand_filelist() from daemon->host_index as
well, and advance it, so that all hosts files get unique indices.

Link: https://lists.thekelleys.org.uk/pipermail/dnsmasq-discuss/2026q4/018636.html
Signed-off-by: David Härdeman <david@hardeman.nu>
---
 src/option.c | 9 +++++++--
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/src/option.c
+++ b/src/option.c
@@ -5742,8 +5742,10 @@ struct hostsfile *expand_filelist(struct
   struct hostsfile *ah, *last, *next, **up;
   struct dirent **namelist;
 
-  /* find largest used index */
-  for (last = NULL, i = SRC_AH, ah = list; ah; ah = ah->next)
+  /* find largest used index. Files found in --hostsdir directories
+     take theirs from daemon->host_index too (dyndir_addhosts()), so
+     start there and advance it below, to keep all indices unique. */
+  for (last = NULL, i = daemon->host_index, ah = list; ah; ah = ah->next)
     {
       last = ah;
       
@@ -5841,6 +5843,9 @@ struct hostsfile *expand_filelist(struct
 	    free(namelist);
 	  }
       }
+
+  if (daemon->host_index < (int)i)
+    daemon->host_index = i;
   
   return list;
 }
